### Docker Lifecycle Management

{"type":"gap","id":"gap-SH-RESTART-DEFAULT","category":"SPEC_IMPL_MISMATCH","priority":"P2","area":"docker_lifecycle","spec_ref":"SH-DEPLOY-CONFIG","spec_file":"13-service-hub-v2.smd","spec_says":"docker.restart: always — shown as the default/example in the spec's .compose.yaml snippet.","impl_has":"ComposeAppProcess.start() defaults restart_policy to 'no' when docker.restart is absent: `restart_policy = docker_cfg.get('restart', 'no')`. Container gets no restart policy unless explicitly declared in .compose.yaml.","impl_file":"hub.py:483","recommendation":"Change the default to 'always' to match spec intent, OR document that restart must be declared explicitly. Given SH-C10 (Docker restart policy honored), the safer fix is to require it to be explicit but emit a WARNING when no restart policy is declared.","status":"open","delta":"NEW"}

{"type":"gap","id":"gap-SH-IMAGE-TAG","category":"UNDERSPECIFIED_NOW_IMPLEMENTED","priority":"P3","area":"docker_lifecycle","spec_ref":"SH-DEPLOY-FLOW","spec_file":"13-service-hub-v2.smd","spec_says":"Build the image. Spec does not prescribe the image tag format.","impl_has":"_build_image() always tags as '{app_id}:latest'. If the app config also specifies docker.image (a remote image), start() prefers docker.image. But if docker.build is set, the built image is tagged {app_id}:latest and start() still uses docker.image (if present) rather than the freshly built tag.","impl_file":"hub.py:414-438,474","recommendation":"After a successful build, start() should use the built tag '{app_id}:latest', not docker.image. Currently if both docker.build and docker.image are set, docker.image wins in start() — the build is wasted. Fix: when docker.build is set, derive image from the build tag, not docker.image.","status":"open","delta":"NEW"}

{"type":"gap","id":"gap-SH-STOP-RUNNING-CHECK","category":"SPEC_IMPL_MISMATCH","priority":"P3","area":"docker_lifecycle","spec_ref":"SH-API-APPS","spec_file":"13-service-hub-v2.smd","spec_says":"POST /stop — stop container (graceful SIGTERM, then SIGKILL).","impl_has":"stop() calls 'docker stop -t 10 {container_name}' regardless of whether the container is actually running. If the container is already stopped, docker stop returns non-zero, and the method returns False — the API then returns success=False even though the desired state (stopped) is achieved.","impl_file":"hub.py:531-557","recommendation":"Before calling docker stop, check if the container is running. If not running, return True (idempotent). If docker stop returns 'No such container' treat as success.","status":"open","delta":"NEW"}

{"type":"area_summary","id":"summary-docker_lifecycle","area":"docker_lifecycle","total_gaps":3,"by_category":{"SPEC_IMPL_MISMATCH":2,"UNDERSPECIFIED_NOW_IMPLEMENTED":1},"by_priority":{"P2":1,"P3":2},"notes":"Docker mechanics are correctly implemented. Gaps are edge-case correctness issues and a subtle build/image precedence bug."}

