### Health Monitoring

{"type":"gap","id":"gap-SH-HEALTH-CYCLE","category":"SPEC_IMPL_MISMATCH","priority":"P2","area":"health_monitoring","spec_ref":"SH-HEALTH","spec_file":"13-service-hub-v2.smd","spec_says":"Hub monitors ComposeApp containers on a 5-second cycle. SH-C7: hub detects stopped container within 10 seconds.","impl_has":"supervise_loop() sleeps 2 seconds between iterations, runs app health checks every 3rd iteration (app_check_counter >= 3). Actual cycle is 2s * 3 = 6 seconds, not 5. Still within the 10-second SH-C7 compliance window, but the stated spec interval is 5s.","impl_file":"hub.py:1017-1033","recommendation":"Either document that the effective cycle is 6s (still SH-C7 compliant) or change the counter threshold to 2 (2s * 3rd check) and sleep 2.5s, giving a 5s cycle. Alternatively restructure with a separate threading.Timer for app checks. The 6s vs 5s difference is not operationally significant but is a spec drift.","status":"open","delta":"NEW"}

{"type":"gap","id":"gap-SH-HEALTH-SCOPE","category":"SPEC_IMPL_MISMATCH","priority":"P1","area":"health_monitoring","spec_ref":"SH-HEALTH","spec_file":"13-service-hub-v2.smd","spec_says":"Hub monitors ComposeApp containers on a 5-second cycle. Implied: all registered apps are monitored.","impl_has":"supervise_loop() only calls app.check_health() for apps where app._running is True. An app whose container exited and was not restarted by Docker (e.g., restart=no, exit_code=1) will have _running=False and will stop being monitored after first failure. Hub accumulates 'dark' apps that had _running set to False and are no longer checked. Status calls still work (check_health() is called from status()), but the background supervision loop is blind to them.","impl_file":"hub.py:1029-1031","recommendation":"Remove the `if app._running:` guard from the supervision loop, OR reset _running=True when check_health() detects the container is actually running (Docker restarted it). The _running flag on ComposeAppProcess conflates 'hub started this' with 'container is currently running', which Docker's restart policy can change independently.","status":"open","delta":"NEW"}

{"type":"gap","id":"gap-SH-HEALTH-WORKLOAD-UNCHANGED","category":"IMPLEMENTED_NOT_SPECIFIED","priority":"P3","area":"health_monitoring","spec_ref":"SH-WORKLOAD","spec_file":"13-service-hub-v2.smd","spec_says":"Workload supervision loop is unchanged from v1 (2-second cycle, hub-managed restarts).","impl_has":"WorkloadProcess.supervise() restarts on exit_code != None, waits restart_delay (5s) synchronously inside the supervision loop. This means a crashing workload delays the entire supervision loop by 5 seconds — including app health checks. With multiple crashing workloads, checks could be delayed significantly.","impl_file":"hub.py:315-340,1022-1023","recommendation":"The restart delay sleep should happen in a background thread, not in the supervision loop. This is a pre-existing v1 issue but is now more impactful because app health checks share the same loop. Low priority for now but worth tracking.","status":"open","delta":"NEW"}

{"type":"area_summary","id":"summary-health_monitoring","area":"health_monitoring","total_gaps":3,"by_category":{"SPEC_IMPL_MISMATCH":2,"IMPLEMENTED_NOT_SPECIFIED":1},"by_priority":{"P1":1,"P2":1,"P3":1},"notes":"The dark-app problem (gap-SH-HEALTH-SCOPE) is the most significant health monitoring gap — apps that fail without a restart policy silently stop being supervised."}

