### Coexistence with Existing Workload Tier

{"type":"gap","id":"gap-SH-COEXIST-COMPLETE","category":"IMPLEMENTED_NOT_SPECIFIED","priority":"P3","area":"coexistence","spec_ref":"SH-DP-1","spec_file":"13-service-hub-v2.smd","spec_says":"Both tiers are first-class. Hub supervises both. No changes to workload tier.","impl_has":"Coexistence is implemented correctly. WorkloadProcess is structurally unchanged. Workloads and apps share the ServiceHub supervision loop cleanly. Both appear in /api/v1/status. The two tiers are properly isolated — a crashing container does not affect workload supervision and vice versa.","impl_file":"hub.py:855-1045","recommendation":"No action required. This area is well implemented.","status":"open","delta":"NEW"}

{"type":"gap","id":"gap-SH-HUB-RESTART-APPS","category":"SPEC_NOT_IMPLEMENTED","priority":"P1","area":"coexistence","spec_ref":"SH-C9","spec_file":"13-service-hub-v2.smd","spec_says":"SH-C9: Hub survives container crash — ComposeApp crashes, hub stays running, reports the crash.","impl_has":"The hub does survive container crashes (it uses subprocess for docker CLI, not in-process). However, because of gap-SH-HEALTH-SCOPE, once a container crashes and Docker does not restart it (_running becomes False), the hub stops monitoring it in the background loop and never logs subsequent state. The crash is logged once at the moment check_health() first detects the stopped state, then silence.","impl_file":"hub.py:617-624","recommendation":"Resolve gap-SH-HEALTH-SCOPE to fix this. Continuous monitoring of all registered apps (regardless of _running flag) ensures the hub always has current state and can report accurately.","status":"open","delta":"NEW"}

{"type":"area_summary","id":"summary-coexistence","area":"coexistence","total_gaps":2,"by_category":{"IMPLEMENTED_NOT_SPECIFIED":1,"SPEC_NOT_IMPLEMENTED":1},"by_priority":{"P1":1,"P3":1},"notes":"Structural coexistence is excellent. The SH-C9 compliance gap is a consequence of the _running flag design flaw, not a separate problem."}

